[{:db/id "q-167324/field-1/d77d44b5097fbbe3c31cbf999f370b945404caabad4bde6b6b7b7c7c9bcdc4d2" :answer/id #uuid "d80af00f-2d29-5293-bdf1-8cb7074bdd95" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4\\cos(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167324/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "4d6a2980-8eee-5a6b-8241-1e37492d1f3d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167324/field-1/d77d44b5097fbbe3c31cbf999f370b945404caabad4bde6b6b7b7c7c9bcdc4d2" :answer-field/choices ["q-167324/field-1/d77d44b5097fbbe3c31cbf999f370b945404caabad4bde6b6b7b7c7c9bcdc4d2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167324" :question/id #uuid "780b7a0c-0e71-5ee6-9bf9-2effe2bd6a24" :question/math-academy-id "q-167324" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that \n\n$$\n\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nApplying this formula and the constant multiple rules, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167324/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0de0627b-2568-5c02-8928-d14344df5a2f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167324"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167325/field-1/0957ec6d647e436213b95fce4d94f342dc6791f310a648804db4c9a4218a432b" :answer/id #uuid "c326e50f-6677-51e5-bcac-fca83ac2b5f0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4x" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167325/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "daf57391-8df7-5295-9026-759a7d8cb9dc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167325/field-1/0957ec6d647e436213b95fce4d94f342dc6791f310a648804db4c9a4218a432b" :answer-field/choices ["q-167325/field-1/0957ec6d647e436213b95fce4d94f342dc6791f310a648804db4c9a4218a432b"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167325" :question/id #uuid "601dd583-1f82-5435-97d3-3e4c9cdd204e" :question/math-academy-id "q-167325" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Computing the first derivative, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}) \\\\  & =8-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 3{x}^{2} \\\\  & =8-2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, computing the second derivative, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{″}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(8-2{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =-4x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167325/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "89f65f58-bd3d-5c3f-b9e0-462d74d75b59"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167325"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167331/field-1/286f252b5fa1a4ba57085c40f32117fc2d0d91f90827ceb8126693f1545fae8e" :answer/id #uuid "f8edfd58-bdbc-5fbb-abb9-7adf13d93e37" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4\\cos(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167331/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "68116713-acce-576f-ac38-3804758b2e18" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167331/field-1/286f252b5fa1a4ba57085c40f32117fc2d0d91f90827ceb8126693f1545fae8e" :answer-field/choices ["q-167331/field-1/286f252b5fa1a4ba57085c40f32117fc2d0d91f90827ceb8126693f1545fae8e"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167331" :question/id #uuid "e934691b-0128-5cb9-9f67-814123c43489" :question/math-academy-id "q-167331" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{e^x(\\cos(x)-\\sin(x))}{8(\\sin(x))^2}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "Recall that\n\n$$\n\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int -4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-4(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+C \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167331/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0cae5c1b-57bd-51f6-a360-db946d25532f"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167331"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167332/field-1/f1bcd5c43d9ff4c196e514c555b67077f239c00eeb64ee745efb6a5d0cc725b5" :answer/id #uuid "2f5a7be5-e731-5c05-8986-72ef7af326a2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^x+12x^2-\\frac{1}{2}x^4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167332/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "3634d3ab-fcde-5241-b1db-c7927325b764" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167332/field-1/f1bcd5c43d9ff4c196e514c555b67077f239c00eeb64ee745efb6a5d0cc725b5" :answer-field/choices ["q-167332/field-1/f1bcd5c43d9ff4c196e514c555b67077f239c00eeb64ee745efb6a5d0cc725b5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167332" :question/id #uuid "1dfafc71-b5a1-5763-aa25-a630d5a7ea69" :question/math-academy-id "q-167332" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{e^x(\\cos(x)-\\sin(x))}{8(\\sin(x))^2}$\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$\n\nEarlier part 7:\n\n$\\int h(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $4\\cos(x)$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\,\\text{d}x\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$+\\,C,\\,$ where $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/worked-solution "We combine the sum and the constant multiple rules, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4f(x)+3g(x))\\text{d}x & =\\int (4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}+3(8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3}))\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int (2{e}^{x}+24x-2{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2\\int {e}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x+24\\int x\\,\\text{d}x-2\\int {x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}+24\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}+C \\\\  & =2{e}^{x}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{2}+12{x}^{2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167332/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "980bb2fb-4bb5-5cbb-aea8-87010493c013"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167332"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167327/field-1/b2dd90a017624a6853616e1d2489b4530f5a962934554cfc87670719591f3745" :answer/id #uuid "91f21f7b-307a-5dcc-90bc-61241fae9f66" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167327/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "110e42fb-6625-51ff-994b-63c558cf8e58" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167327/field-1/b2dd90a017624a6853616e1d2489b4530f5a962934554cfc87670719591f3745" :answer-field/choices ["q-167327/field-1/b2dd90a017624a6853616e1d2489b4530f5a962934554cfc87670719591f3745"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167327" :question/id #uuid "8357b7b8-522c-5fce-b074-39040ffa71f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-167327" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ." :question/worked-solution "When $x=\\pi ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(\\pi ) & =f(\\pi )\\cdot h(\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{\\pi }\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn part 3, we found that the slope of the tangent line to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is $m=2{e}^{\\pi }.$ Therefore, the slope of the normal ${m}^{′}$ is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & ={m}^{′}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-0 & =-\\frac{1}{2{e}^{\\pi }}(x-\\pi ) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{x}{2{e}^{\\pi }}+\\frac{\\pi }{2{e}^{\\pi }}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167327/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55a323ba-f2a7-5a49-abd4-a9812518ef8c"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167327"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167329/field-1/194bfaf0f35909fe6edb77c5e99c5ddb684d5c90daf12a9fc6af706457a3e830" :answer/id #uuid "4730f3b6-5016-5d80-a943-e09b7713066c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\cos(x)" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167329/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "01754fe0-7e74-50e8-af55-c6096ce7339a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167329/field-1/194bfaf0f35909fe6edb77c5e99c5ddb684d5c90daf12a9fc6af706457a3e830" :answer-field/choices ["q-167329/field-1/194bfaf0f35909fe6edb77c5e99c5ddb684d5c90daf12a9fc6af706457a3e830"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167329" :question/id #uuid "714bc6c6-b6f1-57dd-9063-f260edb8be4b" :question/math-academy-id "q-167329" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{e^x(\\cos(x)-\\sin(x))}{8(\\sin(x))^2}$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nGiven that $y=f(h(x)),$ calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n${e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $y$ is a composite function:\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,h=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\n$$\n\nWe now apply the chain rule and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{h}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\,{e}^{-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167329/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "107eaf98-be8c-5fb1-ac4c-948b1e00df0a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167329"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167326/field-1/b68a68812063c0a1cdcf9f8770320fc0d10d82d3ab8b244a3b8b827c108a94cc" :answer/id #uuid "da54e9a4-0ab5-5375-a1b7-eb7fd0417c28" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e^{\\pi}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167326/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "9dd27187-4538-5de6-9dd1-1b90836a38f9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167326/field-1/b68a68812063c0a1cdcf9f8770320fc0d10d82d3ab8b244a3b8b827c108a94cc" :answer-field/choices ["q-167326/field-1/b68a68812063c0a1cdcf9f8770320fc0d10d82d3ab8b244a3b8b827c108a94cc"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167326" :question/id #uuid "1440b9d2-f58f-5c19-9d02-38c9b3ec1544" :question/math-academy-id "q-167326" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nCurrent part:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We differentiate using the product rule. Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$ Then,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the product rule gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}+h\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x} \\\\  & =-2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we now substitute $x=\\pi $ into the formula and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{x=\\pi } & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{\\pi }(-1+0) \\\\  & =2{e}^{\\pi }.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the slope of the tangent line is $2{e}^{\\pi }.$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167326/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee64135e-9b9f-5429-a4df-efa522042b83"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167326"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167328/field-1/4113601dc8d049de22c1d5b8af819e82840ad9ecf17e19e27ec7786bfecd6510" :answer/id #uuid "fa075056-c7c5-5762-8e04-109580a6cc5f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{e^x(\\cos(x)-\\sin(x))}{8(\\sin(x))^2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167328/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "30f5b874-f83e-5d08-9838-e9e7a82c3fbb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-167328/field-1/4113601dc8d049de22c1d5b8af819e82840ad9ecf17e19e27ec7786bfecd6510" :answer-field/choices ["q-167328/field-1/4113601dc8d049de22c1d5b8af819e82840ad9ecf17e19e27ec7786bfecd6510"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167328" :question/id #uuid "37f7bc76-3907-59eb-a60d-c58bc7cdb68a" :question/math-academy-id "q-167328" :question/problem "Consider the following functions: \n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x},\\,g(x)=8x-\\frac{2}{3}{x}^{3},\\,h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\n$$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}x}\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4\\cos(x)$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\n${g}^{″}(x)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-4x$\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nIf a curve $C$ is defined by the equation $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x),$ calculate the slope of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $x=\\pi .$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        Slope $\\,=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $2e^{\\pi}$\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nThe equation of the normal to the curve $y=f(x)\\cdot h(x)$ at the point where $x=\\pi $ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $\\,y=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{\\pi-x}{2e^{\\pi}}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nCurrent part:\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h})\\,=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall that $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x}$ and $h(x)=-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$\n\nWe apply the quotient rule, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{f}{h}) & =\\frac{{f}^{′}(x)\\cdot h(x)-f(x)\\cdot {h}^{′}(x)}{[h(x)]^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})}^{′}\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{′}}{(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)-(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{x})\\cdot (-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{16{sin}^{2}⁡x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)}{8{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167328/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3c547746-49c8-5a4a-85fe-c6b75493a354"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167328"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]}]
